NNAAMMEE
       pamtopnm - convert PAM image to PBM, PGM, or PPM


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppaammttooppnnmm

       [--aassssuummee]

       [_p_n_m_f_i_l_e]

       Minimum  unique abbreviation of option is acceptable.  You may use dou‐
       ble hyphens instead of single hyphen to denote options.   You  may  use
       white space in place of the equals sign to separate an option name from
       its value.


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppaammttooppnnmm reads a PAM image as input and  produces  an  equivalent  PBM,
       PGM, or PPM (i.e. PNM) image, whichever is most appropriate, as output.

       ppaammttooppnnmm  assumes the PAM image represents the information required for
       a PBM, PGM,  or  PPM  image  if  its  tuple  type  is  "BLACKANDWHITE",
       "GRAYSCALE",  or  "RGB"  and  its depth and maxval are appropriate.  If
       this is not the case, ppaammttooppnnmm fails.

       However, you can override the tuple type requirement with  the  --aassssuummee
       option.

       ppaammttooppnnmm  produces  a  PPM  image if the input PAM has depth 3 or 4; it
       produces PGM or PBM if the input PAM has depth 1 or 2.  Whether it pro‐
       duced  PGM  or PBM depends upon the maxval: PBM for 1, PGM for anything
       higher.  The tuple type does not play a role in determining the  output
       type.   You can use Netpbm programs such as ppggmmttooppggmm to generate a dif‐
       ferent PNM output, but remember that Netpbm program  that  expects  PGM
       input will take PBM and so on.

       Note that it's possible for an image which is formally color to in fact
       contain only shades  of  gray  and  for  an  image  which  is  formally
       grayscale to contain only black and white.  This program pays no atten‐
       tion to that; an RGB input image produces a PPM output  image  even  if
       all  the  pixels  are  gray.  But you can use ppppmmttooppggmm to convert a PPM
       that you know is grayscale to the  equivalent  PGM,  and  you  can  use
       ppaammtthhrreesshhoolldd  to  convert  a PGM image you know is black and white to a
       black and white PAM image and then use ppaammttooppnnmm to convert that to PBM.


       As with any Netpbm program that reads PAM images, ppaammttooppnnmm  also  reads
       PNM images as if they were PAM.  In that case, ppaammttooppnnmm's functions re‐
       duces to simply copying the input to the output.  But this can be  use‐
       ful  in a program that doesn't know whether its input is PAM or PNM but
       needs to feed it to a program that only recognizes PNM.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options  ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppaammttooppnnmm recognizes the
       following command line option:



       --aassssuummee
              When you specify --aassssuummee, you tell ppaammttooppnnmm that you  personally
              vouch  for the fact that the tuples contain the same data as be‐
              longs in the channels of a PBM, PGM, or  PPM  file.   The  depth
              must  still conform, though, so to truly force a conversion, you
              may have to run the input  through  ppaammcchhaannnneell  first.   But  be
              careful  with  --aassssuummee.  When you -assume, you make an -ass of u
              and me.




